.docname {Floor Plans as Building Models}
.include {docs}

Building registration accepts one input today: a JSON description of rooms. This page records the decisions for accepting an **architectural drawing** in the same place — what is extracted, what a drawing cannot supply, and where the extraction runs.

For the registration flow this feeds, see [Digital Twin Service Architecture](../architecture/twin-architecture.qd). For the bounded context, [Building Management Context](../domain/building-management.qd).

.box {Design record, not a description of shipped code} type:{note}
    Nothing on this page is implemented yet. It is written first so the format ladder, the calibration model and the extraction contract are settled before the first extractor exists. Phases are listed at the bottom; each one updates this page in its own commit.

---

## The Seam

The format decision lives at exactly one line — `frontend/src/components/modals/creation/RegisterBuildingModal.vue`:

```typescript
const raw = JSON.parse(await file.text())
loadFromJson(raw)
```

Everything downstream of `loadFromJson` is already format-agnostic: the draft, the per-room editor cards, the threshold sliders, the sensor drafts, and `submit()` posting to `/twin/register`. A floor plan becomes a second way to produce the same `BuildingDraft`.

.var {seam}
   - Layer
   - Changes?

.tablebyrows {.seam}
    - - `handleFileSelected` — picks a parser by file type
      - **yes, one branch**
    - - extraction — drawing to `BuildingDraft`
      - **yes, new**
    - - `useBuildingDraft`, room cards, threshold and sensor UI
      - no
    - - `POST /twin/register`, `service::provisioning`, the Kafka handshake
      - no
    - - `schemas/twin-schema`, `domain/building.rs`
      - no

The backend does not learn that floor plans exist. That is the point: a drawing is an *authoring* convenience, and the twin's contract is the room list either way.

### The target is easier than it looks

`domain/building.rs` models a room as an axis-aligned box — `Coordinates { x, y, z }` and `Dimensions { width, height, depth }`. There are no polygons, no wall thicknesses, no door graph. Extraction has to produce **a bounding rectangle and a label**, not reconstruct the architecture.

### The draft editor is the accuracy backstop

Extraction is never trusted. Its output lands in the same reviewable draft a hand-written JSON does, and the user corrects it before anything is submitted. The bar is "close enough to fix in half a minute", not correctness. A phase that chases extraction accuracy instead of improving the editor is solving the wrong problem.

---

## What a Drawing Cannot Tell You

Four of the six room fields are absent from any plan view, and the missing ones are not guessable from the geometry.

.var {gaps}
   - Field
   - On the plan?
   - Where it comes from

.tablebyrows {.gaps}
    - - `x`, `y`, `width`, `depth`
      - yes
      - extracted, after calibration
    - - `z`
      - no
      - `floor_index × floor_height`; one drawing is one floor
    - - `height`
      - never — a plan is a horizontal section
      - default, editable per room
    - - `capacity`
      - never
      - derived from floor area, editable per room
    - - `color`
      - sometimes — a fill
      - carried through when present, else unset

Defaults are stated in one place in code and surfaced in the editor pre-filled, never silently applied. A default the user cannot see is a default they cannot correct.

`capacity` from area is a heuristic with a real ceiling: occupancy per square metre is a function of room *use*, which a plan does not encode. It exists to save typing, not to be right.

---

## Scale Is a Calibration, Not a Heuristic

The twin declares no unit. A drawing carries pixels, or drawing units, or a printed scale bar — nothing that resolves itself. There are exactly two honest sources:

- **Declared by the format.** DXF states `$INSUNITS`. Take it.
- **Declared by the user.** They pick two points on a known span and type the real distance. One number, one drag.

There is no third option. Inferring metres from a bitmap's dimensions, a door's typical width, or a text label that happens to read `1:100` is guessing dressed as convenience, and it fails silently — a building that is uniformly 3× too large still renders, still registers, and is only noticed once someone stands in it.

When the format declares a scale the calibration control is pre-filled, not hidden. Drawings lie about their units often enough that the knob has to stay reachable.

---

## Room Ids Must Be Deterministic

Telemetry keys per-room thresholds by path — `/telemetry/thresholds/peopleCount/buildings/{buildingId}/rooms/{roomId}`. An id that changes between two extractions of the same drawing orphans every threshold attached to the old one.

- Derive the id from the room **label** when the drawing has one, slugified.
- With no label, derive it from the room's position in drawing coordinates — stable under re-export, unlike an array index, which reorders whenever the drawing tool reorders its entity list.
- Collisions get a suffix. Two rooms named `Office` are normal; two rooms with the same id are a data-loss bug.

---

## Format Ladder

Formats are added one at a time, hardest last. Each new format is one extractor plus one arm in the type switch — the contract below is what keeps that true.

.var {formats}
   - Format
   - Mechanism
   - New dependency
   - Scale from

.tablebyrows {.formats}
    - - **SVG** — first
      - `DOMParser`; `<rect>` and `<path>` bounding boxes, nearest `<text>` as the label
      - none — the browser already parses it
      - user
    - - **DXF**
      - ASCII entity list; `LWPOLYLINE` extents, `TEXT`/`MTEXT` labels
      - one parser, or hand-rolled
      - `$INSUNITS`, user-overridable
    - - **PDF, vector**
      - a PDF library down to paths, then identical to SVG
      - a PDF library
      - user
    - - **Raster** — PNG, scanned PDF
      - wall detection, contour extraction, OCR for labels
      - a computer-vision stack
      - user

SVG goes first because it costs nothing and proves the entire path — calibration, floor handling, id minting, the editor round trip — before a single dependency is added. It is rarely what an architect hands over; DXF is. Everything phase 2 and 3 build is reused verbatim when DXF arrives.

Raster is a different discipline and is deliberately last.

---

## The Extraction Contract

One pure function per format, one shape:

```typescript
(file: File, scale: Scale) => Promise<BuildingDraft>
```

Three rules, and they are the whole insurance policy:

- **No Vue, no store, no network.** An extractor reads bytes and returns data.
- **Fixtures are the contract.** Real drawings in, expected `BuildingDraft` JSON out, asserted per format.
- **Dispatch is a switch on file type**, not a plugin registry. Adding a format adds a file and an arm.

Held to those, moving one format to a service later is a swapped call site with the fixtures reused as-is — not a migration.

---

## Where Extraction Runs

**In the frontend**, beside `loadFromJson`. No new service, no route, no image, no auth surface, no `services.json` entry.

The instinct to put it behind an API is usually about accuracy, and for vector formats that instinct is wrong: SVG, DXF and vector PDF parse to the same bounding boxes on either side of the wire. There is no precision to be gained by moving.

The one real asymmetry is **raster**. Wall detection and OCR want a Python and OpenCV ecosystem the browser cannot match, and that is the only condition under which a service earns its deployment. Should it arrive, the endpoint is additive: vector formats keep parsing locally, raster calls out, and the split follows the actual capability rather than a uniform rule.

Deciding that at the end of the ladder means deciding it with real extraction output to judge against.

---

## Deliberate Omissions

- **No polygon rooms.** The twin models axis-aligned boxes. Extracting an L-shaped room's true outline produces geometry nothing downstream can store or render. Its bounding box is the honest answer; splitting it into two rooms is the user's call, in the editor.
- **No raster support in the first ladder rungs.** It is a research problem with a different toolchain, and every phase before it makes the eventual decision better informed.
- **The source drawing is not stored.** The twin persists the derived room list. Retaining the original is document management — retention windows, per-tenant storage, access control — and none of it serves occupancy.
- **One drawing is one floor of one building.** A site plan holding three buildings, or a sheet holding four floors, is split by the user before upload. Detecting the split is inference on top of inference.
- **No plugin architecture for formats.** Four formats is a switch statement.
- **No capacity model beyond area.** Occupancy per square metre by room type is a lookup table someone has to own and keep current. The editor field costs nothing and is always correct.

---

## Phases

.var {phases}
   - Phase
   - Delivers

.tablebyrows {.phases}
    - - 1 — this page
      - format ladder, calibration model, extraction contract, id rule
    - - 2 — SVG extractor
      - pure function plus fixtures; no UI
    - - 3 — wire the seam
      - type branch, calibration control, floor index; SVG works end to end
    - - 4 — acceptance
      - upload, review, submit, building visible
    - - 5 — DXF
      - one extractor, one switch arm, `$INSUNITS`
    - - 6 — PDF, vector
      - paths out of the PDF, then the SVG path
    - - 7 — raster
      - re-decision point: service, or drop

Phases 5 and 6 are additive by construction. Phase 7 is the only one that reopens an architectural question, and it is scheduled last on purpose.

---

## Where This Lives

Proposed, as phase 2 and 3 land.

.var {files}
   - Concern
   - File

.tablebyrows {.files}
    - - type dispatch — the seam
      - `frontend/src/components/modals/creation/RegisterBuildingModal.vue`
    - - draft shape, unchanged
      - `frontend/src/composables/building/useBuildingDraft.ts`
    - - extractors, one per format
      - `frontend/src/composables/building/floorplan/`
    - - defaults, calibration, id minting
      - `frontend/src/composables/building/floorplan/` — shared, not per-format
    - - fixtures — drawings and expected drafts
      - `frontend/src/composables/building/floorplan/__fixtures__/`

Nothing under `backend/` appears in this table, and that is the invariant worth keeping: a floor plan is an authoring format, and the twin's contract is the room list.
